  "query_preview": "..\n\nThe persistent subsidence the last couple of days has resulted in an \nextremely dry airmass across Se Mi. The 00Z KDTX sounding had an \nimpressively dry Precipitable Water Value of 0.22 inches. The \nresident moisture starved airmass will hold in place over Se Mi for \nseveral more days within persistent low to mid level anticyclonic \nflow across much of the eastern US, ensuring a dry forecast with \nmainly clear skies through the end of the week into if not through \nthe weekend. \n\nRising 500mb geopotential heights across the Upper Midwest and Great \nLakes region today into Tuesday will strengthen the low-mid level \nanticyclonic flow across the Great Lakes, with gradually \nstrengthening sfc high pressure becoming centered north of Lake \nHuron tonight, then expanding toward southern Quebec by Tuesday \nafternoon. An elongated ridge axis will extend through Lower Mi, \nensuring weaker winds in comparison to yesterday. Similar thermal \nprofiles and afternoon mixing depths will support daytime highs \ngenerally in the mid 60s to around 70 today through Thursday. The \ndry airmass and light winds will support ideal radiation cooling \npotential, dropping nighttime lows in the upper 30s to mid 40s for \nthe next several nights. With the repositioning of sfc high pressure\nto the northeast of the region, light east-northeast winds off the \nlakes may drive a little higher moisture into the boundary layer, the\nresult of which being a chance for some shallow fog the next couple \nnights.\n\nA progressive northern stream trough forecast to track across \nnorthern Ontario and Hudson Bay Wednesday will drive a weakening \nfront into the northern Great Lakes Thursday. Rapid long wave ridge \namplification forecast across the central US will effectively allow \nsfc high pressure redevelopment across the Great Lakes, eroding any \nremnant sfc frontal feature and sustaining dry conditions. Further \nstrengthening of the surface high into eastern Canada and New England\non Friday will attempt to advect slightly cooler air into Se Mi, \njustifying slightly cooler forecast highs Friday before southerly \nreturn flow trajectories drive a little warmer back into Se Mi for \nthe weekend.",
